  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThrough early mid next week (Wednesday)...\n\nToday: Morning surface and GOES East water vapor/RAP analysis \nindicate mean ridge around 592-594DM centered over the Gulf Coast \nstates. Dry weather and seasonably warm temperatures (highs in \n92-96F range and lows around 60 degrees in east MS to mid to upper\n60s in central to northwest Delta) will persist. Additional \nrecord highs could tie or be eclipsed today. There remains \nefficient afternoon mixing/low humidity into critical levels (25 \nto 35 percent range) for continued Elevated fire danger which \npersist through the weekend. Heat indices will peak in the low to \nmid 90s today. The position of the ridge will continue a \nsoutheasterly fetch of return flow. High pressure will keep winds \nrelatively light. \n\nLate week through early next week (Friday into Tuesday): Fall \nfrontal passage remains likely Friday. An upper low currently over \nthe Rockies will phase with deepening trough axis/upper jet diving \ninto central Canada and southeast into the northern Plains to Mid MS \nValley. This will help an amplified meridional and positively tilted \ntrough. Stout, dry cold front will drive dewpoints falling into the \nmid to upper 30s (36-45F). Cannot rule out a rouge shower but \noverall majority areas will remain dry. Cool down remains likely \nthis weekend. Highs will be seasonably warm Friday (72-78F in the \nDelta and 83-91F in southeast of the Delta), with lows in the low to \nmid 60s (61-66F) that morning and lows that night mid to upper 40s \nto low 50s north of Interstate 20 (45-50F) to mid to upper 50s (50-\n53F) to the south. With continued drought stressed vegetation, this \nstrong gusty front will likely increase fire danger concerns. The \ncold front will move into the Delta around daybreak Friday, \nInterstate 20 around midday and out of the area by early evening. \n1030-1040mb surface high building will build east out of the lee \nside of the Rockies through the Mid West, leading to 6-8mb pressure \ngradient and resultant gusty gradient wind of 20-25mph sustained and \nwind gusts reaching 30-35mph at times. This will be a concern, so \ncontinue to monitor for any fire related headlines. A Fire Weather \nWatch remains in effect across the region. The Storm Prediction \nCenter continues to highlight a Day 2 Elevated fire weather outlook \nfor areas west of Interstate 55. There will efficient mixing but \nhumidity may be slightly higher than today, so Elevated graphic \nstill fits and will persist through the weekend. As the trough digs \nacross the Eastern Seaboard and cuts off the Atlantic seaboard, \nridge over the western Gulf will amplify, with the axis over the \nArkLaTex. Seasonable and pleasant fall temperatures will build \nareawide into Saturday and Sunday (highs of 69-74F Saturday and 70-\n78F Sunday and lows of 41-45F north of Interstate 20 to 46-50F to \nthe south). Quiet and dry weather will persist next week, with an \nOmega block pattern developing (trough over the Baja California and \nsouthwest US and another just off the Carolina coast). Low level \nreturn flow and deep dry moisture profiles will lead to moderating \ntemperatures (low 80s early week to mid to upper 80s by late week) \nand lows becoming more seasonable. With continued drought, stressed \nvegetation and afternoon humidity nearing critical levels by mid \nweek (25 to 35 percent), fire danger will likely persist. Holding \noff introducing anything for next week. /DC/",
